From ee833ce17e6d14ce83959ffd0ba81d791cf68992 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 16:51:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] pbr: a rule whose tunnel is down follows that client's
 Killswitch

Policy routing realised BLOCK as an unconditional lower-priority `prohibit`:
whenever a target tunnel went down, every rule pointed at it failed closed,
whatever the owner had asked for on that client's own VPN page. The firmware
already has a switch for exactly this decision - vpn_clientN_enforce /
wgcN_enforce, the Killswitch VPN Director obeys - and two answers to one
question is one too many.

P3 now reads it per target: Killswitch on keeps the `prohibit` (fail closed),
Killswitch off omits it and the traffic falls back to the WAN. BLOCK is still
realised solely as `prohibit`, and nothing else about rule generation moves.

Saving a Killswitch restarts vpnrouting, so that restart now calls
reaper_pbr_regen(), which regenerates the COMMITTED rules in place. It is
deliberately not start_reaper_pbr(): that unlinks the draft, which would
discard an apply the owner is still confirming.

The page stops claiming fail-closed for everything. The CGI reports each
client's Killswitch (wks/oks); the chip on a rule whose client is off says
whether that traffic is blocked or uses the WAN; the target column names the
interface; a rule that bypasses acceleration for the whole LAN is badged LAN;
and Apply keeps its overlay up until the Keep / Revert bar takes over.

Metal (RT-BE96U, 2026-09-11): wgc5_enforce=0 gives "9010 lookup wgc5" alone and
`ip route get 1.1.1.1 mark 0xa0000` goes via the WAN; =1 adds "9110 prohibit"
and the same command is denied. Both from the nvram toggle plus
`service start_vpnrouting0`, with no Policy Routing apply - which is the regen
path end to end. Teardown verified clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
